Yes. The pattern you’re extracting is how a past event keeps a vote in what happens next. An emotion can keep an event in attention, give it a meaning, and make some next moves feel more available than others. You can graft that pattern onto a story, an interface, or an organization.

Emotion	What it can keep in view	What it can prompt	Where it can become a trap
Guilt	“I did something that hurt someone.”	Acknowledge it, make amends, change the behavior.	Every attempt at repair becomes another occasion to replay the offense.
Shame	“What does this say about who I am or how they see me?”	Hide, explain yourself, or try to regain standing.	Every new action gets interpreted through the old label.
Grief	“Someone or something I loved is gone.”	Remember, reach for others, reorganize life around the loss.	A ritual or institution starts treating any change as disloyalty.

The transferable structure is event → interpretation → repeated cue → next move → what changes the loop. Grief exposes a limit in the pattern. Some events can’t be repaired. You can keep the second mug and still make room for something new in the cupboard.

In software, a failed import can stay visible until the user retries it or dismisses it. The useful part of guilt’s pattern is that an event remains actionable. The trap appears when the warning survives the repair and keeps demanding attention.

In fiction, the person who once ran away returns and stays when it matters. One witness says, “He came back.” Another says, “He always comes back after the hard part.” Same act, two readings. That’s your braid in motion. Whoever’s reading presides gets to decide whether the character’s next act can change his name.

In an institution, a memorial can carry an obligation forward after everyone who lived through the event is gone. The obligation might be to keep telling the story, support the people affected, or change the practice that caused the loss. Each choice gives the past a different kind of authority.

For the Pantheon, I’d test it with three questions. What does the loop keep alive? Who gets to decide what it means? What new event can change what happens next? The third is where a tool can turn into a trap. One person can write the character, and an audience can keep casting them in it long after they’ve changed.